{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b632-a368-725f-ac3b-6a1a4e290e30","session_id":"01a1b631-833e-7cce-99be-733d57cd5e95","message":{"id":"01a1b632a368725fac3b6a18a52a14a1","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So manual-baseline / environment-blocked are only mentioned in test-strategy item 5, not implemented as states in AC-070 or design. AC-070 only has waivers for real-smoke. Q-2 confirmed \"manual-baseline and environment-blocked ... 不计 pass，豁免单独列出\". The implementation uses release/waivers.json for real-smoke only; no environment-blocked state. But test-strategy item 5-6 references them as applicable only to real env limits. Hmm, AC-070 confirms \"某条 AC 在 baseline 上只有 skip 时阻断\" and \"real-smoke 报告...否 waived\". So \"manual-baseline\" ≈ real-smoke + waiver, and \"environment-blocked\" not implemented. Q-2 might be satisfied partially.\n\nLet me check AC-038 (WSL) — baseline real-smoke, waiver. That maps to manual-baseline. environment-blocked has no mechanism; but maybe not needed since real-smoke waiver covers. Still Q-2's decision is not fully implemented, but the test-strategy records the口径.\n\nNow, the decision is about the *minutes and decisions*, not the blueprint. The minutes' 4.1 items are user-confirmed (Q-1..Q-5). The 4.2 items are drafts; decisions.json marks all default_adopted. The query asks \"是否可以接受这些决策\" and lists D-1..D-8 in the decisions block. So I judge D-1..D-8.\n\nGiven user's latest answer says \"六条已按可判据改造完成\" and the verification shows:\n- AC-005 ✓ rewritten.\n- AC-073 ✓ rewritten.\n- registry exemption ✓.\n- F-04 ✗ partially — write_scope removed, but outputs still assign a1-quota [fixed] sections to T-006/T-007 not in write_scope; design §5 dual owner; defects.json single owner_task; no per-owner subsection validation. The claim \"T-006/T-007 的 write_scope 与 outputs 已移除该路径\" is false for outputs: outputs still contain \"defects/a1-quota 的预测部分 [fixed]\" and \"a1-quota 的 draft 部分 [fixed]\".\n- F-07 ✓.\n- TB reclassification ✓ (test-strategy §6).\n- D-1/D-7/D-8 anchors ✓ in design §10.\n\nAnd critical: TB-1, TB-3, TB-6 are still unresolved in the graph/design, despite being labeled \"must fix before M1\". The minutes doesn't claim they're fixed; it claims they're reclassified as spec defects. So the claim \"六条已按可判据改造完成\" refers to the six items in my prior rejection, not to TB-1..6. The six items were: (1) F-02→AC-073, (2) F-05→AC-005, (3) Q-3 vs AC-005, (4) registry exemption, (5) F-04/F-07, (6) D-1 fields. So \"六条\" = my 1-6 numbered points. In that framing:\n1 ✓, 2 ✓, 3 ✓, 4 ✓, 5 partial (F-04 not fully), 6 ✓ (design §10 anchor, though not user-confirmed).\n\nSo one item is only partially done, and the claim \"outputs 已移除该路径\" is factually wrong.\n\nDoes this warrant reject? The F-04 residual creates an actual implementation deadlock: T-006/T-007 outputs require writing a file outside write_scope; check-write-scope will fail. This is exactly the kind of \"图冲突\" I demanded fixed. I'd say reject with a precise, minimal remedy, or accept_with_notes with mandatory note. Given the pattern (twice rejected for claiming closure), and the fact that F-04 is a concrete graph conflict that blocks M1, reject seems consistent. But is reject proportionate? The instruction says reject \"表示你反对，需要用户处理\". There are 4 concrete remaining issues, two of which block M1 (TB-3 CI set equality, F-04 write deadlock), plus TB-1 and TB-6. The decisions themselves (D-1..D-8) are fine; it's the \"改造完成\" claim that's inaccurate.\n\nHmm. The user asks \"现在独立确认最终纪要与决策\" with revisions. The final decision list includes D-1..D-8. My job: can I accept the decisions? If I reject, I'm rejecting the minutes' accuracy/closure claim, not the decisions per se. The verdict options: accept_with_notes = accept but with remarks. Since the decisions are acceptable and the remaining issues are implementation-graph details that must be fixed before M1, accept_with_notes might be appropriate. But my previous accept condition was explicit; one of four conditions is half-met. If I accept, the record will say \"accepted\", and the graph conflict remains for M1. Notes can make it conditional.\n\nLet's weigh: The minutes' final decisions D-1..D-8 are sound and now anchored. The blocking F-02/F-05 are genuinely closed in the graph (AC-073/AC-005). The remaining TB-1/3/6 and F-04 are open but were supposed to be fixed before M1; the minutes/test-strategy explicitly records them as \"必须 M1 前修图\". So accepting the decisions with notes that M1 must not start until these four are fixed is coherent — it doesn't silently discard blocking because it's explicitly recorded as a precondition.\n\nBut the user's answer says \"六条已按可判据改造完成\", and item 4's claim is false. If I accept, I should correct that in notes. The verdict \"accept_with_notes\" allows notes. Does accept_with_notes mean I accept the decisions, with corrections? Yes.\n\nHowever, there's a deeper issue: D-1..D-8 are marked in minutes 4.2 as drafts not confirmed by user, but decisions.json marks them default_adopted with confirmed_by_user kind default. The minutes says \"默认采纳即构成用户确认\" for Q-1..5; decisions.json applies that to D-1..8. The minutes' 4.2 says they're drafts. This is an internal inconsistency in the record. The user's actions (anchoring in design §10) suggest de facto adoption. For the confirmation, I can note that D-1..D-8 should be recorded as default-adopted (per decisions.json) or sent for explicit confirmation; either way not silently \"user-confirmed\". Acceptable with note.\n\nWhat about Q-2 implementation (manual-baseline/environment-blocked) — AC-070 only has waivers for real-smoke; no environment-blocked state and no separate table? test-strategy item 6 says 豁免单独成表; AC-070 text doesn't mention manual-baseline/environment-blocked. The confirmed decision #2 says \"两者均不计 pass，豁免单独列出\". Test-strategy §6 records this. AC-070 requires \"release/waivers.json ... 对非 real-smoke AC 的豁免条目导致阻断\". So manual-baseline maps to real-smoke waiver; environment-blocked isn't implemented. Q-2's environment-blocked is for WSL discover etc. — but AC-038 WSL is real-smoke. Maybe environment-blocked isn't needed. Still, the decision says introduce both; the graph implements only waiver. Minor gap, worth a note.\n\nAlso Q-3 confirmed says \"仅冻结 contracts/ 内版本化契约及注册表快照，并覆盖 schema 键名和事件类型\" — but AC-005 freezes contracts/spec/** + contracts/golden/**, and registry.snapshot is exempt from hash. Q-3's literal \"注册表快照\" freeze is overridden by the explicit exception (which the user's Q-7 answer says takes precedence). The minutes 4.1-3 says \"仅冻结 contracts/ 内版本化契约及注册表快照\" — which now contradicts the registry exemption. So 4.1-3 should be corrected: registry snapshot is versioned + compareRegistry, not hash-frozen. The user's item 3 says the exception overrides literal. So minutes 4.1-3 is stale. That's a real inconsistency in the minutes text (not decision). Accept_with_notes should flag it.\n\nAlso 4.1-1 still includes \"身份证据统一由 CLIWorker 输出，AgentLine 不再重复硬判\" — my prior objection #3. The minutes file line 76 still contains it, unchanged! The user's latest answer item 6 says D-1 fields anchored in design. But the minutes 4.1-1 still claims user-confirmed content beyond Q-1's original answer. My prior rejection demanded fixing that. It's still unfixed in the minutes text. Hmm — the query's minutes 4.1-1 says: \"身份证据统一由 CLIWorker 输出，AgentLine 不再对「未提供身份」重复硬判。（Q-1）\". The Q-1 answer only covered asserted/provisional/switch/unknown. So 4.1-1 still overclaims. The D-1 default is now default_adopted in decisions.json, and the user anchored it in design, which arguably covers it. But the minutes still attributes it to Q-1. So the record remains inaccurate.\n\nGiven all this, I think reject is more defensible than accept_with_notes? Let's think about what the user wants: they've iterated twice; they want closure to enter Build. The remaining items are:\n(a) minutes 4.1-1 overclaims D-1 (still).\n(b) 4.1-3 says freeze registry snapshot, conflicting with the exception (new).\n(c) F-04 half-fixed: outputs still claim a1-quota [fixed] sections not in write_scope; design §5 dual owner; no per-owner validation.\n(d) TB-1/TB-3/TB-6 still unfixed in graph/design; TB-3 will break ubuntu/macos.\n(e) Q-2 environment-blocked not implemented in AC-070.\n\n(c) and (d) directly block M1. My prior accept conditions included (c). So I should reject, with a minimal list of edits (text/graph only). But I've rejected twice; the third rejection must be precise and actionable, not just \"not satisfied\".\n\nAlternatively accept_with_notes: the decisions are acceptable, and the notes carry the mandatory pre-M1 fixes. But accept_with_notes semantics = \"接受但有备注\" — that seems appropriate when the decisions themselves are fine but residual implementation items must be tracked. The difference from reject is whether user needs to handle before confirmation. The instruction: \"reject 表示你反对，需要用户处理\". Since the minutes still has false claims (4.1-1, 4.1-3) and F-04 graph conflict, and these need user handling, reject seems right.\n\nBut hold on — is it my role to keep blocking on blueprint text accuracy when the user has decided to go to Build and treat F-02/F-05 as M1 acceptance? The user's Q-6 answer explicitly says \"接受现有设计直接进入 Build，这 2 条 blocking 不作为设计收口前提\". I accepted the direction. My condition was to make the ACs judgeable. That's done. The remaining TB-1/3/6 were already declared \"must fix before M1\" — the user isn't claiming they're fixed in the graph. If they're recorded as pre-M1 obligations, then the minutes is accurate enough. So maybe accept_with_notes is the right call, noting:\n- AC-005/AC-073 verified as strengthened.\n- F-04 claim is inaccurate: outputs still reference a1-quota [fixed]; must remove from outputs or add per-owner subsections + write_scope.\n- TB-1/TB-3/TB-6 remain open; M1 must fix them before execution (TB-3 will fail ubuntu/macos).\n- 4.1-1 overclaims D-1; 4.1-3 conflicts with registry hash exemption; correct the minutes.\n- manual-baseline/environment-blocked only in test-strategy; AC-070 implements waivers only; environment-blocked has no state.\n\nHmm, but if I accept_with_notes while saying \"the minutes contains false statements and a graph conflict that blocks M1\", that's contradictory. accept_with_notes usually means decisions accepted, notes are caveats. If the caveats include \"must fix before M1\", that's fine — it's not rejecting the decisions.\n\nLet me examine the instruction wording: \"请判断是否可以接受这些决策。... reject 表示你反对，需要用户处理；accept_with_notes 表示接受但有备注。\" So the object is 决策 (D-1..D-8). I can accept them. The notes can state the residual conditions. That fits accept_with_notes.\n\nBut my prior stance was \"不能第三次把 blocking 静默消解\". Is accepting with notes a silent dissolution? No — the notes explicitly say F-04/TB-1/TB-3/TB-6 remain open and are M1 preconditions. It's not silent.\n\nYet the minutes claims \"六条已按可判据改造完成\". If I accept, the final record might present that as accepted fact. The notes can correct it. The user will see it.\n\nI lean accept_with_notes. But let me double-check the key claims once more to be fair:\n\n1. AC-005: allowed set includes contracts/spec/** and contracts/golden/**. But wait — Q-3 said \"仅冻结 contracts/ 内版本化契约及注册表快照，并覆盖 schema 键名和事件类型；不冻结 lib/bin/config\". AC-005 freezes spec/golden; registry exempt. Good.\n   However, AC-005 says \"[repro] 段的 sha256\" registered in defects.json — but the mutation canary uses \"已修复的 v0.1 变体\". Where does that variant live? Not specified (test/characterization/canary/** in write_scope). Fine.\n   Potential issue: check-frozen-files \"由 check-fast 执行\" — check-fast runs all check-*.mjs, so yes.\n   Potential issue: freezing contracts/golden/** + contracts/spec/** in check-frozen-files AND check-golden-frozen in AC-073 — duplicate but consistent.\n\n2. AC-073: manifest.json per-file sha256; check-golden-frozen in check-fast; check-write-scope reports changes outside registered list and outside write_scope. But how does check-write-scope know task write_scope? From task-graph? The graph is in .collab, and check-no-collab forbids references to .collab in scripts/tests/contracts/docs/package.json. Hmm! check-write-scope needs the task write_scope data, which lives in .collab/blueprint/.../task-graph.json, but check-no-collab forbids any reference to `.collab`. That's a catch-22: the checker can't read the graph. Unless write_scope is transcribed into contracts/spec... but AC-073 says check-write-scope reports \"落在任务 write_scope 之外的变更\". Where does it get write_scope? No file exists. This is a real unresolved design gap in the new AC. Interesting. It could be implemented by a committed copy of write scopes (e.g., contracts/spec/write-scopes.json), but AC-073 doesn't specify it and T-004 write_scope doesn't include such a file. So check-write-scope is unimplementable as specified without violating check-no-collab or adding an unspecified source. That's a legitimate technical concern.\n\nActually, T-002/T-004 could hardcode write scopes? But then guard drift. Not specified. This is a new gap introduced by the fix. Worth noting, possibly blocking.\n\n3. test-strategy item 2: \"[repro] 段 sha256 由 defects.json 登记并校验\" — but the [repro] content is in the same file as [fixed], which owners modify. How does check-defects compute the sha256 of a section? Extract text between markers; deterministic. Fine.\n\n4. F-04: as noted, outputs still claim dual sections. Let me verify T-006/T-007 outputs precisely — yes lines 883, 930. And design line 143. And no per-owner subsections. So F-04 not closed.\n\n5. TB-1: AC-061 vs AC-072 conflict persists; test-strategy still says \"须写明...\" (obligation). design line 13 \"health 也会报告\" vs AC-061 \"degraded\". So TB-1 open.\n\n6. TB-3: AC-001 unchanged; no \"已加载\" definition. test-strategy doesn't specify. TB-3 open.\n\n7. TB-6: design §0 line 18 vs §4 line 107. Open.\n\n8. Q-2: environment-blocked not implemented as a state; AC-070 only waivers. test-strategy mentions it but no AC. Minor.\n\n9. D-1..D-8: decisions.json marks all default_adopted with confirmed_by_user; minutes 4.2 marks drafts. Inconsistent record.\n\nGiven the number of residuals and that two block M1, I think the honest verdict is reject (with precise reasons), because the final minutes asserts completion while 4 of 6 TB plus F-04 remain, and 4.1 still overclaims. But is rejecting the *decisions* fair? The decisions are fine. Hmm.\n\nThe user asks \"是否可以接受这些决策\" — I can accept the decisions D-1..D-8. The verdict is about the decisions, not the blueprint's graph. If I reject, it's because the minutes' claim of completion is false; but the decisions themselves are acceptable. reject means \"我反对，需要用户处理\". What would the user need to handle? The minutes text corrections and the graph conflicts. Those are not \"decisions\" per se but the basis for acceptance.\n\nIn Studio protocols, confirming the minutes means accepting the record as-is. If the record contains false claims, confirm = endorse falsehood. So reject is justified.\n\nBut the prompt's final instruction: \"请判断是否可以接受这些决策。\" and notes. The decisions D-1..D-8 themselves I can accept, with notes that some supporting claims are inaccurate and pre-M1 fixes remain. accept_with_notes seems semantically designed for this: accept the decisions, note the caveats. If I reject, the user must revise again; but the remaining issues are real and would block M1, so revision is warranted.\n\nLet me consider proportionality: The user's last answer claims \"六条已按可判据改造完成\". Verified 5 of 6, with #4 half. Two of the six (F-02/F-05) were the actual blocking; both fully closed. Q-3 conflict resolved in AC-005 (graph) but design prose stale. D-1/D-7/D-8 anchored. The remaining TB-1/3/6 are known and explicitly assigned to pre-M1. So the direction is sound and the main blocking is closed. A reject for the a1-quota outputs + design prose might be seen as pedantic, but TB-3 is not 